WEBVTT

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:11.760
This is Izakaya Nobu.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:16.490
It's an ordinary izakaya, but 
there is one extraordinary thing.

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:14.990
<b>Nobuyuki Yazawa (32)
 Owner of the izakaya "Nobu"</b>

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:24.600
Our front door opens into 
a parallel universe!

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.000
<b>Shinobu Senke (23)
 Waitress at the izakaya "Nobu"</b>

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:28.010
Isekai Izakaya

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:28.010
Japanese Food From Another World

00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:33.010
<b>The Burglar</b>

00:00:38.470 --> 00:00:41.380
Please hurry, Father!

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:42.450
<b>Nikolaus (24)
Palace Guard
Ancient City Aitheria</b>

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:42.920
There was a burglary!

00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:47.020
Nikolaus-san, if you keep rushing like that,

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:50.790
<b>Edwin (70)
Deacon
Ancient City Aitheria</b>

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:50.780
we won't even be able to solve 
cases that we should be able to.

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:54.920
<i>Why does this old man have 
to come along just now?</i>

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:57.730
Of all places, we're talking
about Izakaya Nobu!

00:00:57.730 --> 00:00:59.660
That place is very dear to me!

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:01.370
<b>Izakaya Nobu</b>

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:03.240
Huh?

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:04.810
What's happened?

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:07.050
<b>Izakaya Nobu</b>

00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:07.060
They seem to be regularly opened.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:08.890
That's strange.

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:10.910
Maybe it was a false report?

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:12.900
Well, let's go in and see.

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:14.440
Then we'll talk.

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:15.590
Yes.

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:18.120
Welcome!

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:18.960
Welcome.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:21.020
Party of two, am I right?

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:22.510
Go ahead and take a seat at the counter.

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:27.060
Our appetizer for you, Nikolaus-san, 
is a bonito tataki.

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:27.070
<b>Bonito Tataki</b>

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:27.070
<b>Aromatic yet crisp.
It pairs well with garlic!</b>

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:28.820
Bonito... tataki?

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:32.840
The bonito is lightly seared, 
then cut like sashimi.

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:35.850
Why do all that work?

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:41.840
Back in the day, raw bonito was banned 
by nobles who feared food poisoning.

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:45.570
But everyone wanted to keep 
eating bonito as sashimi.

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:49.500
The solution was to sear only the 
outside, and make it seem cooked.

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:50.590
<b>*Differing opinions exist</b>

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:51.090
I see.

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:54.280
I suppose everyone wants to eat tasty things.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:55.990
That's all it is.

00:01:56.350 --> 00:01:57.920
Now let's see.

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:06.430
<i>The flesh is still so tender and aromatic!</i>

00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:08.820
<i>There's no fishy smell at all.</i>

00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:12.510
<i>Tastes fresher than ordinary sashimi.</i>

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:14.110
<b>Fizz fizz</b>

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:20.740
It pairs really well with 
the whatsontap, too.

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:25.600
This reishu is wonderful.

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:29.250
See? This is a great restaurant.

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:33.280
Oh, wait. I almost forgot.

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:36.820
Father, what about work?

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:42.820
Rejecting something put before you 
is rejecting the will of God.

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:46.580
Shinobu-chan, today I come not 
as a customer, but on a mission.

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:48.570
I see. What is it?

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:51.810
Did you have a burglar enter 
these premises today?

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:55.190
We had a report earlier in the guard room.

00:02:55.190 --> 00:02:57.590
Father here has come along as well.

00:02:57.610 --> 00:03:00.710
Well, I don't know what you mean.

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:09.230
If you try to tell a falsehood before 
Father, it could cause trouble for you later.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:13.660
"Burglar" is saying too much...

00:03:20.710 --> 00:03:22.680
Good morning!

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:24.070
Huh?

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:26.920
Is Taishou still asleep?

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:29.690
But his sandals aren't here, 
so maybe he's out shopping.

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:33.150
I'll finish cleaning while
I wait for him to come back.

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:38.940
Huh?

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:40.670
This is open.

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:44.850
<i>What does this mean?</i>

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:47.950
<i>I'm sure I locked this 
with the key last night.</i>

00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:49.760
Could it be a burglar?

00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:51.310
Burglar?!

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:53.900
Could they still be inside?

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:00.590
Is someone in there?!

00:04:00.590 --> 00:04:02.790
I know you're there!

00:04:06.610 --> 00:04:08.480
I-I'm really sorry.

00:04:10.710 --> 00:04:12.450
Wh-What?

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:15.310
What's... Who are you?

00:04:15.850 --> 00:04:18.310
My name is Eva.

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:19.700
I'm so sorry!

00:04:20.290 --> 00:04:22.050
My name is Shinobu.

00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:24.840
Eva-chan, how did you get in here?

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:27.710
The door to this place was open.

00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:32.730
When I peeked in, I saw a man running water.

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:36.350
Oh, Taishou was using the water, I guess.

00:04:36.350 --> 00:04:39.820
Then, when the man had gone away,

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:44.230
I really, really wanted the piece of 
metal where the water comes out.

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:46.360
Oh, you mean the faucet.

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:47.850
Fau-cet?

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:50.310
It's true that water does 
come out when you turn it,

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:52.230
but you need more than just 
that for water to come out.

00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:55.740
Huh? Is that so?

00:04:55.740 --> 00:04:57.520
I-I'm sorry.

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:01.070
Jeez, this is all because 
Taishou leaves the door open.

00:05:01.070 --> 00:05:02.930
Please let me atone for this!

00:05:02.930 --> 00:05:03.620
Huh?

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:07.170
I have committed the 
despicable act of stealing.

00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:10.260
So please let me atone for it.

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:13.440
But you didn't actually 
take anything yet, so...

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:17.660
I believe the crime already happened 
when I had the desire to steal it.

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:20.630
That's what I learned in church as well.

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:25.260
But it's really scary if you're 
taken by the palace guard.

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:30.020
That is fine. If you report me 
to them, I'll go willingly.

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:33.280
Wow, still so small but already so mature.

00:05:34.390 --> 00:05:35.910
So that's what's up.

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:38.550
<b>Eva (14)
 Resident - Ancient City Aitheria</b>

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:40.400
You're the burglar?

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:41.970
Y-Yes.

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:43.770
Why are you stealing things?

00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:46.330
You can get as much water 
as you want from the well.

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:47.780
Or from the river or the canal.

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:50.080
Well, that's...

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:52.890
You can get it, but you can't drink it.

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:55.400
Unless you boil it once.

00:05:57.630 --> 00:05:59.980
Then she should just boil it.

00:05:59.980 --> 00:06:01.260
That's not easily done.

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:03.990
This year especially, the 
price of firewood is high.

00:06:03.990 --> 00:06:06.320
That's right. Our customers have 
been saying that the price

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:09.900
of firewood is high this year because 
spring still hasn't arrived yet.

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:11.770
I-I'm sorry.

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:12.920
Hmm.

00:06:13.750 --> 00:06:15.750
But if we let you off the hook now,

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:18.660
we have no guarantee that you 
won't steal from another place.

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:20.520
I will guarantee that!

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:24.790
We've decided to have Eva-chan 
wash dishes at our place.

00:06:24.790 --> 00:06:27.340
Lately, it's been getting 
tough for me to handle both

00:06:27.340 --> 00:06:28.800
waiting on customers and washing dishes.

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:31.060
You're saying Nobu is hiring this child?

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:31.820
Yes!

00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:34.300
That's basically it.

00:06:34.300 --> 00:06:37.020
Well, what should I do now?

00:06:37.030 --> 00:06:41.410
Nikolaus-san, the two people who 
were affected are saying this.

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:45.520
I don't think there's any need to make 
this case any more serious than this.

00:06:45.790 --> 00:06:49.900
Well, if you say so, Father.

00:06:49.900 --> 00:06:53.380
Thank you, Nikolaus-san, Edwin-san!

00:06:53.380 --> 00:06:55.840
Th-Thank you!

00:06:57.020 --> 00:06:59.950
I guess that's settled.

00:06:57.030 --> 00:06:59.960
<b>Reishu</b>

00:06:57.030 --> 00:06:59.960
<b>Fragrant Japanese rice wine, enjoy it cold</b>

00:07:03.460 --> 00:07:06.840
With that said, there's one 
more case that remains.

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:07.700
Ahh.

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:10.210
Doesn't the church decree

00:07:10.210 --> 00:07:13.110
that you are supposed to only have 
simple meals until the spring equinox?

00:07:13.110 --> 00:07:16.070
W-Well, you could say that's the rule.

00:07:16.070 --> 00:07:19.600
Is that really okay? A man of the 
cloth taking the rules so lightly?

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:22.700
Wh-What might you be talking about?

00:07:25.250 --> 00:07:26.750
Shinobu-chan, what's up?

00:07:26.750 --> 00:07:29.990
I mean, Edwin-san is a regular here.

00:07:29.990 --> 00:07:30.640
Right?

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:31.500
What?!

00:07:32.610 --> 00:07:34.830
I did think it was weird.

00:07:34.830 --> 00:07:37.840
I mean, how else would he know about reishu?

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:41.730
And it was weird that you 
knew his name anyway!

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:46.010
He always comes in early, so he's not 
here when the palace guards come in.

00:07:46.010 --> 00:07:50.530
Edwin-san's appetizer is always shutou.

00:07:50.530 --> 00:07:51.600
Shutou?

00:07:51.620 --> 00:07:56.390
<b>Shutou</b>

00:07:51.620 --> 00:07:56.390
<b>The solid texture is irresistible</b>

00:07:51.860 --> 00:07:56.100
It's pickled innards of the 
bonito that you had earlier.

00:07:56.390 --> 00:07:58.360
Since it's makes the sake taste so good,

00:07:56.390 --> 00:08:01.130
Shutou (Sake Thief)

00:07:58.360 --> 00:08:00.710
the name suggests that it steals sake.

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:01.130
<b>*Differing opinions exist</b>

00:08:01.880 --> 00:08:03.160
This is it, huh?

00:08:03.160 --> 00:08:04.160
Let's see.

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:07.770
<b>Droop</b>

00:08:10.900 --> 00:08:14.770
<b>Wave crash</b>

00:08:11.740 --> 00:08:14.760
<i>This is an intense saltiness!</i>

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:17.430
But try this.

00:08:20.550 --> 00:08:22.550
<b>Clear</b>

00:08:23.810 --> 00:08:26.450
The saltiness of the shutou is washed away

00:08:26.450 --> 00:08:30.120
and the tastiness of the sake 
is spreading in my mouth!

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:30.660
<b>Clean clear</b>

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:30.660
<b>Clean clear</b>

00:08:34.760 --> 00:08:35.940
I can't stop!

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:37.220
See?

00:08:37.780 --> 00:08:40.590
Edwin-san, here's your usual.

00:08:42.060 --> 00:08:44.310
This! This is what I've been waiting for!

00:08:44.310 --> 00:08:46.180
It's heshiko and mamakari.

00:08:46.180 --> 00:08:48.480
Heshiko? Mamakari?

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:50.690
Heshiko is mackerel pickled in rice bran.

00:08:50.690 --> 00:08:53.040
Mamakari is small sardinella 
pickled in vinegar.

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:55.190
It's the trinity of Nobu's delicacies!

00:08:55.640 --> 00:08:57.570
The trinity of delicacies!

00:08:57.570 --> 00:08:59.180
This heshiko...

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:04.090
<b>Heshiko</b>

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:04.090
<b>The well-ripened umami
spreads throughout</b>

00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:11.510
This saltiness has such body to it!

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:18.780
This is it! This delicate balance 
between that and the sake's sweetness!

00:09:18.780 --> 00:09:20.810
<b>Mamakari</b>

00:09:18.780 --> 00:09:20.810
<b>The sourness from being pickled 
in vinegar boosts your appetite!</b>

00:09:18.810 --> 00:09:20.790
And then the mamakari.

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:29.580
This is a totally different, sour taste,

00:09:29.580 --> 00:09:33.050
but this pairs absolutely 
perfectly with the reishu!

00:09:33.290 --> 00:09:36.300
Mamakari's "mama" is derived from rice.

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:37.720
When you eat this with 
rice, you always run out,

00:09:37.720 --> 00:09:39.930
so you have to borrow more 
from your friend Kari.

00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:41.690
<b>*Differing opinions exist</b>

00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:41.690
<b>Another
bowl,
please!</b>

00:09:39.930 --> 00:09:41.680
Hence mamakari.

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:45.570
They probably pair well since 
reishu is made from rice as well.

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:50.820
This is mine. I won't share with anyone.

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:54.570
How can a man of the cloth 
have so little compassion?!

00:09:54.570 --> 00:09:57.410
That is that, and this is this!

00:09:57.410 --> 00:10:00.010
I only came along because
I was worried when I heard

00:10:00.010 --> 00:10:02.570
that this place was involved 
in some sort of commotion.

00:10:03.740 --> 00:10:07.900
It seems like I do have to 
report a burglary after all.

00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:09.920
Ahh, my shutou!

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:13.880
This is a felon who stole the sake.

00:10:13.880 --> 00:10:16.760
The palace guard will make it their 
responsibility to secure him.

00:10:16.760 --> 00:10:19.390
Along with his other delicious 
accomplices, too.

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:24.680
No... Please have mercy!

00:10:29.190 --> 00:10:31.290
Next up is Nobu Plus!

00:10:31.190 --> 00:10:33.020
<b>Nobu</b>

00:10:33.420 --> 00:10:35.030
<b>Nobu</b>

00:10:35.030 --> 00:10:40.060
<b>Narrator
 Teiyu Ichiryusai</b>

00:10:35.050 --> 00:10:38.060
That staple for sashimi or tataki, bonito,

00:10:38.060 --> 00:10:40.800
has yet another delicious 
way of being prepared!

00:10:41.150 --> 00:10:46.750
Ryuuta Kijima is going to put his own 
spin on the dishes featured in Nobu!

00:10:43.330 --> 00:10:47.070
<b>Ryuuta Kijima Creates Nobu Dishes</b>

00:10:47.460 --> 00:10:50.990
Eva-chan made an appearance. 
She came to steal that lump of metal.

00:10:48.890 --> 00:10:55.630
<b>Eva</b>

00:10:50.990 --> 00:10:51.880
She's so courageous.

00:10:51.880 --> 00:10:54.750
I wanted to give her a lump of metal.

00:10:54.750 --> 00:10:55.630
You're so nice to young girls!

00:10:55.630 --> 00:11:00.210
That kindness, to hire someone 
who came in as a burglar.

00:10:55.980 --> 00:11:03.620
<b>Ryuuta Kijima</b>

00:10:56.450 --> 00:11:03.620
<b>Grew up in a household where both his grandmother and his mother were culinary researchers,
so he got into cooking at an early age. Now he's a culinary researcher who's popular
for his simple but serious recipes and his fun presentation.</b>

00:11:00.210 --> 00:11:01.680
It was yet another good episode.

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:03.640
Hey, how about we get to cooking?

00:11:03.640 --> 00:11:05.880
First, Taishou served that tataki.

00:11:05.880 --> 00:11:08.310
That's what gave me an idea for a dish that

00:11:07.480 --> 00:11:14.290
<b>Easily palatable bonito dishes even 
for people who don't like fish</b>

00:11:08.310 --> 00:11:12.260
could be good for people who 
don't like bonito or raw fish.

00:11:12.260 --> 00:11:14.280
So that's what I'd like to show today.

00:11:14.280 --> 00:11:15.820
Sounds good!

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:19.270
<b>Fried Rare Bonito
 [Ingredients (serves 2)]
 Bonito   300 g
 Cabbage   1/8th
 Perilla   2 leaves

00:11:15.820 --> 00:11:19.280
Fried rare bonito? You mean 
you're frying the bonito?!

00:11:19.280 --> 00:11:24.680
I want to make fried bonito. 
It's fried, but inside it's still raw.

00:11:24.680 --> 00:11:25.450
Ah, that's why it's rare.

00:11:25.450 --> 00:11:27.160
That's the kind of cutlet 
I want to show you.

00:11:27.160 --> 00:11:29.540
Thinly sliced cabbage and perilla.

00:11:29.540 --> 00:11:32.950
I'll show you a lifehack for this.

00:11:32.950 --> 00:11:35.520
Just sneak in the perilla between 
the leaves of the cabbage.

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:37.620
Then you can just cut them together.

00:11:38.040 --> 00:11:44.480
Since you want them to have the same 
thickness, cutting them together is definitely easier.

00:11:42.580 --> 00:11:46.650
<b>If you stack the cabbage and perilla 
leaves, they'll come out nice and thin</b>

00:11:44.860 --> 00:11:46.660
Hey, that's a nice idea!

00:11:46.660 --> 00:11:48.220
Next, the bonito.

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:49.980
Let's get it cut.

00:11:49.980 --> 00:11:55.650
Since I want the thickness to be as even 
as possible, I'll cut off the thin part.

00:11:55.650 --> 00:11:58.130
You can eat that as sashimi if you like.

00:11:58.130 --> 00:12:01.540
I want to have slices that are about 
10 cm, but I guess half will do.

00:12:01.540 --> 00:12:02.060
Roger!

00:12:02.060 --> 00:12:03.070
I just cut it in half like this.

00:12:06.190 --> 00:12:09.440
Salt it rather generously.

00:12:09.070 --> 00:12:11.740
<b>Salt the bonito generously</b>

00:12:10.180 --> 00:12:14.710
I see! You set the base with the 
salt, then you roll it in flour,

00:12:11.740 --> 00:12:21.750
<b>Fried Rare Bonito
 Batter -  Coat in flour (as needed), then egg (as 
needed), then Panko breadcrumbs (as needed)</b>

00:12:14.710 --> 00:12:19.330
dip it in the egg, and finalize 
the preparation by covering

00:12:19.330 --> 00:12:21.750
all sides with the panko breadcrumbs!

00:12:21.750 --> 00:12:25.250
When you've added the panko, please 
let it sit and rest for a bit.

00:12:25.250 --> 00:12:28.380
That way you can prevent the panko 
from going all over the moment

00:12:25.690 --> 00:12:33.920
<b>After battering, wait 5 minutes 
to let it get attached</b>

00:12:28.380 --> 00:12:30.550
you put this into the oil.

00:12:30.550 --> 00:12:33.920
The final product will also be a 
bit more crispy and light then.

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:37.580
About the oil. This time we're 
going to have it be quite hot.

00:12:37.580 --> 00:12:39.750
About 190 degrees Celsius.

00:12:40.380 --> 00:12:44.090
It went over 190. We're going to fry 
them in one go at around 190 degrees.

00:12:40.810 --> 00:12:44.610
<b>Fry in one go at a temperature 
of above 190 degrees Celsius</b>

00:12:44.090 --> 00:12:48.880
Wow, that's way hotter than pork cutlet 
or croquettes! You just go for it!

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:52.380
Once the surface has a nice 
fox color, it's ready.

00:12:52.820 --> 00:12:56.220
<b>Frying time is roughly 1 minute. Once 
the surface has a fox color, it's good</b>

00:12:53.380 --> 00:12:55.590
So fast, I love it!

00:12:58.370 --> 00:13:02.020
The inside is rare, like tataki!

00:13:02.030 --> 00:13:05.670
Once it's fried, you have to cut it 
immediately or the residual heat will cook it.

00:13:02.460 --> 00:13:09.470
<b>Once fried, immediately cut so 
the inside doesn't get cooked.</b>

00:13:05.670 --> 00:13:08.760
So it's best to cut it immediately 
and eat it while it's crunchy.

00:13:08.760 --> 00:13:09.810
Sounds good.

00:13:09.810 --> 00:13:14.230
Soy sauce, wasabi, mayonnaise, or 
cutlet sauce are all valid options,

00:13:09.830 --> 00:13:21.710
<b>Fried rare bonito</b>

00:13:14.230 --> 00:13:16.380
but I think just salt is tasty, too.

00:13:16.380 --> 00:13:18.580
The outside is crispy, the inside is tender.

00:13:18.580 --> 00:13:20.090
I think it's great fit for sake, too.

00:13:20.090 --> 00:13:21.390
Please do try making this dish.

00:13:21.390 --> 00:13:22.140
Will do!

00:13:22.140 --> 00:13:25.890
First the cutlet, then the umami 
just hits you right after!

00:13:25.890 --> 00:13:28.510
This custom bonito cutlet 
recipe has an fun texture.

00:13:28.510 --> 00:13:32.560
I'm sure even people who don't 
like raw fish would enjoy this!

00:14:47.970 --> 00:14:58.010
<b>Next Time...
After Work Tonjiru</b>

00:14:48.800 --> 00:14:54.000
Next time on "Isekai Izakaya"
aka "Izakaya Nobu in Aitheria."

00:14:49.770 --> 00:14:51.270
<b>Steam steam</b>

00:14:54.350 --> 00:14:57.500
Please sample the tasty tales.
